# INC-0002 — SSH Authentication Flood Against rhel01

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| Incident | INC-0002 |
| Severity | SEV-3 (single host, no compromise) |
| Detected by | Splunk alert "SSH brute-force attempt (rhel01)" |
| Affected host | rhel01 (192.168.20.20, VLAN 20 Servers) |
| Source | 192.168.99.2 (VPN client, authorised lab workstation) |
| Handler | Corey Armstrong |
| Attack started | 2026-10-04 16:52 |
| Detected | 2026-10-04 16:56 |
| Contained | 2026-10-04 17:00 |
| Validated | 2026-10-04 17:10 |
| Status | Resolved |

> **Lab exercise.** Failed SSH authentications were generated deliberately from an authorised workstation to test end-to-end detection and response. No real attacker was involved. The source IP is lab-internal.

## Metrics

| Metric | Value |
|---|---|
| Time to detect (TTD) | ~4 min (attack 16:52 → detection 16:56) |
| Time to contain (TTR) | ~8 min (detection 16:56 → block 17:00, validated 17:10) |
| Failed auth events | 64 from a single source in ~35 seconds |

## 1. Detection

Splunk flagged a single source IP exceeding the failed-authentication threshold on rhel01.

```
index=linux sourcetype=linux_secure ("Failed password" OR "Failed publickey" OR "Connection closed by authenticating user" OR "Invalid user")
| rex "(?:from|user \S+ )(?<src_ip>\d+\.\d+\.\d+\.\d+)"
| stats count AS failures, values(user) AS users_tried, min(_time) AS first, max(_time) AS last by src_ip
| where failures >= 5
| convert ctime(first) ctime(last)
| sort - failures
```

Result: `192.168.99.2` — **64 failures**, user `baduser`, 16:56:08 → 16:56:43. *(Screenshot 52)*

## 2. Investigation

| Question | Finding |
|---|---|
| One source or many? | Single source: 192.168.99.2 |
| Which account(s)? | `baduser` — does not exist on rhel01 |
| Succeeded at any point? | No — zero "Accepted" events for that source |
| How was it reaching SSH? | Over the OpenVPN tunnel (192.168.99.0/24), which is allowed to rhel01:22 by design |
| Compromise indicators? | None — no accepted logins, no new sessions, no sudo activity from that source |

rhel01 is key-only (`PasswordAuthentication no`), so every attempt was refused at authentication. The flood was noise, not a breach — but the volume is exactly what a real brute-force looks like, so it was treated as an incident.

## 3. Root cause

An authorised VPN client generated a burst of failed SSH authentications (lab test). The underlying exposure being exercised: any host that can reach the tunnel can hammer rhel01:22, and without a detection + block, a real attacker could do the same unnoticed.

## 4. Containment

Blocked the single offending source at the host firewall:

```bash
sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --add-rich-rule='rule family="ipv4" source address="192.168.99.2/32" drop'
sudo firewall-cmd --reload
sudo firewall-cmd --list-rich-rules
```

Scoped to `/32` deliberately — blocking the whole VPN subnet would have cut off legitimate admin access.

## 5. Validation

| Check | Before block | After block |
|---|---|---|
| `ssh baduser@192.168.20.20` from 192.168.99.2 | "Permission denied" (reached sshd) | **Connection timed out** (dropped at firewall) *(Screenshot 53)* |
| Admin SSH from an allowed source / Proxmox console | Working | Still working |
| New failures in Splunk from 192.168.99.2 | — | None after 17:00 |

The change from "Permission denied" to "timed out" confirms packets are now dropped before sshd.

## 6. Follow-up / corrective actions

- [ ] **Planned** — Install and configure `fail2ban` (or a firewalld equivalent) so repeated SSH failures are auto-blocked without manual action.
- [ ] **Planned** — Splunk alert tuning: add a second, higher-severity rule for failures from *outside* the VPN/management ranges.
- [x] Detection alert "SSH brute-force attempt (rhel01)" in place and tested.
- [ ] **Decision needed** — time-box the manual `/32` block or move it into a managed blocklist, so stale drop rules don't accumulate.

## 7. Lessons learned

- Key-only SSH stops the login but **not the noise** — detection still matters.
- A host firewall `drop` is a fast, reversible containment step; scoping to `/32` avoids collateral lockout.
- "Permission denied" vs. "connection timed out" is a clean, demonstrable proof that a network-layer block is working.
- Console access (Proxmox) is the safety net when editing firewall rules remotely.